WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Electricity runs everything in an HVAC system. If you don't understand it, you
can't diagnose anything.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Electricity is the language of HVAC systems. Every component — compressor, fan
motor, blower, control board, thermostat — runs on electricity. This lesson
teaches the three fundamental electrical properties: Voltage (V) is electrical

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Voltage (V): Electrical pressure measured in volts. Residential HVAC uses 24V
(control), 120V (blower), and 240V (compressor, heat strips).

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Amperage (I): Electrical current measured in amps. Compare measured amps to the
nameplate rating to determine if a motor is working correctly.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Resistance (Ω): Opposition to current flow measured in ohms. A good wire has
near-zero ohms. An open circuit has infinite ohms.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: When a compressor nameplate says RLA 14.0
and you measure 18.5 amps, you know something is wrong — dirty condenser coil,
low voltage, or failing compressor. Without understanding amps, you cannot make

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: Always measure voltage BEFORE measuring resistance — never
measure resistance on an energized circuit. And — A motor drawing MORE than RLA
is overloaded. A motor drawing LESS than RLA may have a mechanical problem.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

